%S A092697 1,105263157894736842,1034482758620689655172413793,102564,142857,
%T A092697 1016949152542372881355932203389830508474576271186440677966,
%U A092697 1014492753623188405797,1012658227848,10112359550561797752808988764044943820224719
%N A092697 For 1 <= n <= 9, a(n) = least number m such that the product n*m is obtained merely by shifting the rightmost digit of m to the left end (a finite sequence).
%C A092697 This is the least n-parasitic number. A k-parasitic number (where 1 <= k <= 9) is one such that when it is multiplied by k, the product obtained is merely its rightmost digit transferred in front at the leftmost end.
%C A092697 Also sometimes called Dyson numbers or least n-transposable numbers. - _Robert D. Rosales_, Aug 11 2026
%D A092697 C. A. Pickover, Wonders of Numbers, Chapter 28, Oxford Univ. Press UK 2000.
%H A092697 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parasitic_number">Parasitic number</a>.
%H A092697 P. Yiu, <a href="http://math.fau.edu/yiu/RecreationalMathematics2003.pdf">k-left-transposable integers</a>, Chap.18.2 pp. 168/360 in 'Recreational Mathematics'
%F A092697 a(n) = min_{k=n..9}(k*(10^(ord(10,(10n-1)/gcd(k,10n-1)))-1)/(10n-1)). - _Robert D. Rosales_, Aug 11 2026
%e A092697 102564 is 4-parasitic because we have 102564*4=410256.
%e A092697 For n=5: 142857*5=714285. - Dzmitry Paulenka (pavlenko(AT)tut.by), Aug 09 2009
%t A092697 Table[With[{k = Range[n, 9]}, Min[k (10^(If[# == 1, 1, MultiplicativeOrder[10, #]] & /@ ((10 n - 1)/GCD[k, 10 n - 1])) - 1)/(10 n - 1)]], {n, 9}] (* _Robert D. Rosales_, Aug 11 2026 *)
%o A092697 (Python)
%o A092697 from sympy import n_order; from math import gcd
%o A092697 [min(k * (10**(n_order(10, m) if (m := (10*n - 1) // gcd(k, 10*n - 1)) > 1 else 1) - 1) // (10*n - 1) for k in range(n, 10)) for n in range(1, 10)] # _Robert D. Rosales_, Aug 11 2026
